# sindy_physics.py
"""Ensemble-SINDy discovery of the sparse physical vector field f_physics.

This module is the *offline* half of the APHYNITY-style decomposition

    xdot = f_physics(x; theta_p) + f_NODE(x, h; theta_a)

It discovers which physical terms are supported by the data, estimates their
coefficients, and writes a JSON spec that ``gru_ode.HGVPhysics`` consumes
directly. Nothing here depends on torch, so discovery can run on a CPU box
without the training stack.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE LIBRARY IS PHYSICS-STRUCTURED, NOT POLYNOMIAL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
A generic polynomial library (the SINDy default) is actively harmful here.
The failure being targeted is out-of-distribution: extrapolations gain energy,
exceed launch speed and fly below ground. A polynomial in altitude fitted on
in-distribution data diverges the moment it leaves that data - it has exactly
the pathology we are trying to remove. So the library contains four terms,
each of which extrapolates correctly by construction:

  T1 drag        a = -k_d * rho * V * v                       [k_d in 1/m]
       Vector form, not v^2. Always antiparallel to velocity, so it
       dissipates energy at every state in R^n, including states no training
       trajectory ever visited. Lower altitude -> larger rho -> more
       deceleration. This is the single term buying the OOD property.

  T2 lift        a_h = -k_l * rho * V * v_u * vhat_h
                 a_u = +k_l * rho * V * V_h                   [k_l in 1/m]
       Perpendicular to velocity within the vertical plane, so a . v == 0
       identically. Lift can therefore shape the trajectory (skip-glide)
       but can *never* add energy, at any state. Enforced by construction,
       not by a penalty.

  T3 gravity     g = k_g * g0 * (R/(R+alt))^2                 [k_g dimensionless]
  T4 curvature   a_u = +k_c * V_h^2 / (R + alt)               [k_c dimensionless]
       Both are known kinematics. They are still regressed, because the
       recovered k_g and k_c are a *frame-contract check*: correct frame and
       units give k_g ~= 1 and k_c ~= 1. A recovered k_g of 0.4 means the
       altitude channel, the unit convention or the frame handedness is
       wrong. Given that this project has already been bitten by an
       asymmetric transform pair, that diagnostic is worth its cost.

Density is rho_hat = exp(-alt_geo / H) with the sea-level rho0 folded into
k_d and k_l. This makes the altitude datum irrelevant to identifiability:
exp(-(alt + c)/H) = exp(-c/H) * exp(-alt/H), so any constant offset between
the frame's altitude channel and true altitude is absorbed by the
coefficient. Only H itself must be estimated, which is done by a 1-D scan
outside the sparse regression.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY ENSEMBLE, NOT PLAIN SINDy
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Plain STLSQ gives a point estimate and no notion of confidence. With ~120
trajectories and derivative estimates from finite differences, a term can be
selected or dropped on noise alone. Ensemble-SINDy (Fasel et al.) bootstraps
the rows and bags the library, then reports a *median* coefficient and an
inclusion probability per term. Terms below the inclusion threshold are
switched off structurally - their coefficient is pinned at zero and not
trained - which is what keeps f_physics sparse and interpretable through the
subsequent APHYNITY fine-tuning.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
UNITS: THIS RUNS IN PHYSICAL UNITS, NOT SCALED UNITS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
The training pipeline StandardScaler-normalises the state. Every function
here expects metres and metres/second. Un-scale before calling. Passing
scaled data yields a spec whose k_g is not ~1, which the frame-contract
check will catch.

